     Checked against developer.android.com (Accessibility in Compose; Semantics; merging and clearing) on 2026-10-25. -->

<!-- @opening -->
<p class="story-lead">A TalkBack user opens an inbox. A custom row handles taps with pointerInput/detectTapGestures but exposes no accessibility click action. Sender and subject are separate stops, and double-tapping either cannot open the message. The screen looks finished; to this user it does not work.</p>

Accessibility services do not see pixels or composables. They see the **semantics tree**: what each element is, what state it has and what it can do. Compose builds a good tree for built-in components, and custom components must describe themselves. This chapter covers labels, merging, state and errors, traversal, large text, touch targets, and how to test all of them.

<!-- @after Accessibility Model :: visual -->
### Two trees for one screen

<figure class="viz"><div class="viz-cols" style="--cols:2"><div class="col"><span class="col-title">Composition (what draws)</span><div class="node"><strong><code>Row</code> (clickable)</strong></div><div class="node"><strong><code>Icon</code></strong><small>decorative</small></div><div class="node"><strong><code>Text</code> sender</strong></div><div class="node"><strong><code>Text</code> subject</strong></div></div><div class="col"><span class="col-title">Semantics (what is announced)</span><div class="node is-key"><strong>One merged node</strong><small>"Asha · Lunch on Friday?" · role Button · click action</small></div><div class="node" data-state="ghost"><strong>Icon</strong><small>hidden: <code>contentDescription = null</code></small></div></div></div><figcaption>Merging turns several drawn children into one thing a user can find, understand and activate.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after Merging Semantics :: animation -->
### Swipe through one email row, before and after

<figure class="stepper"><span class="stepper-title">What TalkBack focuses and says</span><div class="stage viz-cols" style="--cols:2"><div class="col"><span class="col-title">Pointer-only row</span><div class="node" data-el="u1"><strong>Stop 1</strong><small>image has a focusable semantics node but no useful name</small></div><div class="node" data-el="u2"><strong>Stop 2</strong><small>"Asha"</small></div><div class="node" data-el="u3"><strong>Stop 3</strong><small>"Lunch on Friday?"</small></div><div class="node" data-el="u4"><strong>Double-tap</strong><small>nothing: the text has no action</small></div></div><div class="col"><span class="col-title">Merged row</span><div class="node" data-el="m1"><strong>Stop 1</strong><small>"Asha, Lunch on Friday?, Button"</small></div><div class="node" data-el="m2"><strong>Double-tap</strong><small>opens the message</small></div></div></div><script type="application/json" class="steps">[
{"label":"First swipe","caption":"The faulty custom image exposes a focusable semantics node with no useful label. A decorative Icon with contentDescription = null would instead contribute no semantics. The corrected row exposes one labelled click action.","add":["u1","m1"],"state":{"u1":"error","m1":"done"}},
{"label":"Keep swiping","caption":"The pointer-only row has no accessibility click action, so the sender and subject remain separate stops with no open-message action.","add":["u2","u3"],"state":{"u2":"warn","u3":"warn"}},
{"label":"Try to open","caption":"Double-tap invokes the focused node’s semantics action, not an arbitrary pointerInput handler. The corrected clickable row supplies the action and merges eligible child semantics.","add":["u4","m2"],"state":{"u4":"error","m2":"done"}},
{"label":"The fix","caption":"Use clickable with an appropriate role and action; it merges eligible descendants. Remove the custom decorative image semantics (an ordinary Icon can use contentDescription = null). Keep independent child actions separate.","state":{"u1":"ghost","u2":"ghost","u3":"ghost","u4":"ghost"}}
]</script><figcaption>Merge when children form one logical control; keep them separate when each child has its own action.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after Production Failure Modes :: check -->
**What does the user hear?** Decide before opening each answer.

<details><summary>An <code>IconButton</code> with <code>Icon(Icons.Default.Close, contentDescription = null)</code>.</summary>

"Button", with no name: the only content was hidden. Give the icon a purpose-based description such as "Close".
</details>

<details><summary>A donut chart drawn on a <code>Canvas</code> with no semantics.</summary>

Nothing at all. Add `clearAndSetSemantics` with a description and progress information that carries the same meaning.
</details>

<details><summary>A text field shows red error text below it, with no semantics change.</summary>

The error may be read as a separate text, unconnected to the field. Mark the field with `isError` and set its `error(…)` semantics so the problem is announced with the field.
</details>
